  "voting_summary": "Washington is a vote-by-mail state: nearly all ballots arrive by mail or drop box, though in-person voting sites are available, and results publish as combined totals only. Ballots postmarked by election day are accepted until certification, nearly three weeks later. Polls close at 11 p.m. Eastern; counties release a large pre-processed dump shortly after closing, then a batch each afternoon for one to two weeks as late mail verifies. Later batches lean Democratic, so close races tighten leftward over the week after election night.",
